WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000
Transcriber: Bob Prottas
Reviewer: Leonardo Silva

00:00:07.113 --> 00:00:13.380
﻿Hello! My name is Magnus Walker.
I was born in 1967 in Sheffield, England.

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:19.362
I left school at 15,
and I came to America at the age of 19.

00:00:19.362 --> 00:00:22.966
Well, 8 weeks ago,
I didn’t know what a TED talk was,

00:00:22.966 --> 00:00:25.779
and to be honest,
I’m not quite sure why I’m here today.

00:00:25.779 --> 00:00:29.053
But I do appreciate the opportunity
to be with you guys

00:00:29.053 --> 00:00:33.911
and share my story, my journey,
and my hopes, and my dreams.

00:00:33.911 --> 00:00:39.150
You know, having left school at 15,
for me, I didn’t really have any future.

00:00:39.150 --> 00:00:42.041
Well, I came to America 28 years ago,

00:00:42.041 --> 00:00:44.755
and that represented
the land of opportunity for me.

00:00:44.755 --> 00:00:49.250
And in those past 28 years,
I’ve been able to build three things:

00:00:49.250 --> 00:00:53.547
a successful clothing company,
a film location business,

00:00:53.547 --> 00:00:57.257
and also restored, raced, driven,

00:00:57.257 --> 00:01:00.283
and collected quite a lot
of classic Porsches.

00:01:00.283 --> 00:01:05.544
Porsche is a passion for me, and I’ll talk
about that in detail in a little bit.

00:01:05.544 --> 00:01:08.812
But all 3 of those things
share one common bond.

00:01:08.812 --> 00:01:10.627
I had no education in them.

00:01:10.627 --> 00:01:14.034
I didn’t really think I was
going to end up in that particular field.

00:01:14.034 --> 00:01:16.202
I didn’t really know where I was going.

00:01:16.202 --> 00:01:19.944
But all three of those things
have a common thread, common bond.

00:01:19.944 --> 00:01:23.187
And that common bond for me
really was freedom.

00:01:23.187 --> 00:01:28.670
Freedom to do whatever I wanted to do,
and a dream sort of to be able to --

00:01:28.670 --> 00:01:33.439
I suppose, live my life to the fullest,
and do whatever I wanted to do.

00:01:33.439 --> 00:01:36.994
So coming to America really was a journey,

00:01:36.994 --> 00:01:40.144
and I’ll start my journey in 1977.

00:01:40.144 --> 00:01:43.494
1977 in England was sort of a special year.

00:01:43.494 --> 00:01:46.254
We had this Punk Rock thing going on,

00:01:46.254 --> 00:01:49.014
and we also had
this Royal Jubilee thing going on.

00:01:49.014 --> 00:01:52.586
But for me it was a start
of a very memorable moment.

00:01:52.586 --> 00:01:57.155
My father took me to
the London Earls Court Motor Show in 1977.

00:01:57.155 --> 00:02:01.473
Back then I fell in love with this car.
It was a white martini Porsche.

00:02:01.473 --> 00:02:03.795
Now any kid growing up
anywhere  in the world

00:02:03.795 --> 00:02:07.704
in the late '70s, early '80s,
chances are you probably had a choice

00:02:07.704 --> 00:02:12.012
of three cars on your wall:
Porsche Turbo, Ferrari Boxer,

00:02:12.012 --> 00:02:13.953
or Lamborghini Countach.

00:02:13.953 --> 00:02:16.049
For some reason I chose Porsche.

00:02:16.049 --> 00:02:19.197
I even wrote a letter to Porsche
when I was 10 years old,

00:02:19.197 --> 00:02:22.160
and essentially said to them,
"I want to design for Porsche."

00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:25.710
They wrote back to me, and said:
"Call us when you’re a little bit older,"

00:02:25.710 --> 00:02:28.963
which I thought was pretty funny,
and they sent me a sales brochure,

00:02:28.963 --> 00:02:32.288
and 35 years later,
they’d end up writing me a letter back

00:02:32.288 --> 00:02:35.034
but I’ll get to that story
a little bit later on.

00:02:35.034 --> 00:02:37.298
So I am this young kid
growing up in Sheffield.

00:02:37.298 --> 00:02:41.646
Sheffield’s a grim northern steel town
as shown by this picture right here.

00:02:41.646 --> 00:02:44.594
There wasn’t necessarily
many Porsches on the road.

00:02:44.594 --> 00:02:48.082
So I filed that dream away;
I had the poster on the wall,

00:02:48.082 --> 00:02:51.859
and I was watching Motorsports
as kid, also in 1977.

00:02:51.859 --> 00:02:55.792
England had James Hunt.
He was a Formula One world champion.

00:02:55.792 --> 00:03:00.059
And we also had Barry Sheene. He was
a two-wheel motor GP champion back then.

00:03:00.059 --> 00:03:02.992
So even though I didn’t grow up
with any sort of fancy cars --

00:03:02.992 --> 00:03:06.332
my father was a salesman,
I grew up in a working-class background --

00:03:06.332 --> 00:03:08.841
I did have this dream early on,

00:03:08.841 --> 00:03:12.051
and somehow this dream involved Porsche.

00:03:12.051 --> 00:03:17.061
Back then, I was also a pretty competitive
middle-distance cross-country runner,

00:03:17.061 --> 00:03:21.592
sort of a solo sport guy, and I used
to love getting out there, and running.

00:03:21.592 --> 00:03:23.133
I became quite competitive.

00:03:23.133 --> 00:03:25.574
I joined this club called
the Hallamshire Harriers,

00:03:25.574 --> 00:03:29.734
and this guy called Sebastian Coe
set quite a few world records,

00:03:29.734 --> 00:03:34.324
and ran at the ‘80, and ‘84 Olympic Games,
and he was sort of inspirational to me.

00:03:34.324 --> 00:03:37.042
Around that same time,
I also fell in love

00:03:37.042 --> 00:03:39.723
with something called heavy metal music.

00:03:39.723 --> 00:03:42.352
Growing up in Sheffield,
there were a lot of rock bands.

00:03:42.352 --> 00:03:45.870
It may have been sort of a slightly
depressed, grim, northern city,

00:03:45.870 --> 00:03:48.433
but there was a lot of music,
and a lot of fun.

00:03:48.433 --> 00:03:50.409
So, I fell in love with Porsche,

00:03:50.409 --> 00:03:53.019
doing some middle distance
cross country running,

00:03:53.019 --> 00:03:55.089
fell in love with heavy metal music,

00:03:55.089 --> 00:03:59.986
and I had decided by the end
of the fifth year I would leave school.

00:03:59.986 --> 00:04:04.847
I left school in 1982, basically
with two O levels, and no real future.

00:04:04.847 --> 00:04:07.853
By that time, I’d also figured out
I could go drink in a pub.

00:04:07.853 --> 00:04:11.396
So for some reason that was great
for going to clubs and having fun,

00:04:11.396 --> 00:04:15.481
but wasn’t so good for a middle-distance
cross-country runner, and an athlete.

00:04:15.481 --> 00:04:17.186
So that sort of faded away.

00:04:17.186 --> 00:04:20.531
But a little thing
that stuck with me was the passion,

00:04:20.531 --> 00:04:23.459
and sort of the drive,
and I think to this day,

00:04:23.459 --> 00:04:26.299
those memorable moments
earlier on are still with me.

00:04:26.299 --> 00:04:28.738
I’m still running around.
I’m still chasing around.

00:04:28.738 --> 00:04:31.362
I’m still running after my goal.

00:04:31.362 --> 00:04:34.396
So I bummed around on the dole
for a little bit

00:04:34.396 --> 00:04:36.616
doing odd jobs, and stuff like that.

00:04:36.616 --> 00:04:39.016
And I started to hear
this comment quite a lot:

00:04:39.016 --> 00:04:41.136
"Cut your hair, and get a real job."

00:04:41.136 --> 00:04:44.397
Well, I was on the dole,
working construction, living at home,

00:04:44.397 --> 00:04:48.765
no car, taking the bus to places,
and for a year or two, that was okay.

00:04:48.765 --> 00:04:53.354
By the time I turned 17 that I decided,
"Okay I’m not going to cut my hair

00:04:53.354 --> 00:04:55.535
but maybe I should think about
getting a job."

00:04:55.535 --> 00:04:59.135
So I actually took a yearlong leisure
and recreation study course

00:04:59.135 --> 00:05:01.102
sports management at a college.

00:05:01.102 --> 00:05:03.353
I heard about this thing
called Camp America.

00:05:03.353 --> 00:05:04.443
What was camp America?

00:05:04.443 --> 00:05:06.866
I didn’t know, but found that Camp America

00:05:06.866 --> 00:05:11.160
sent kids to work on a summer camp
in the United States of America.

00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:14.537
Growing up as a kid, of course,
I watched a lot of American TV.

00:05:14.537 --> 00:05:18.583
Most of the shows I loved centered
around action and cars, Starsky and Hutch,

00:05:18.583 --> 00:05:22.088
Dukes of Hazzard, CHiPs.
So I had this American Dream.

00:05:22.088 --> 00:05:25.197
It involved Evel Knievel;
anyway, long story short,

00:05:25.197 --> 00:05:28.912
I took this leap of faith,
and I applied to Camp America.

00:05:28.912 --> 00:05:31.092
It was a little bit of a strange feeling.

00:05:31.092 --> 00:05:33.194
I’ve had these
strange feelings in the past,

00:05:33.194 --> 00:05:35.955
and somehow when my gut
tells me to do something,

00:05:35.955 --> 00:05:39.816
generally it’s a good thing.
Hence go on your gut feeling.

00:05:39.816 --> 00:05:43.105
So by pure luck I supposed,
I was accepted into Camp America,

00:05:43.105 --> 00:05:48.696
got on a flight to New York,
took a Trailways bus from New York.

00:05:48.696 --> 00:05:52.040
That’s the bus I took to Detroit.
Detroit was great.

00:05:52.040 --> 00:05:54.790
It was similar to Sheffield,
former industrial city.

00:05:54.790 --> 00:05:59.020
It also happened to be this sort
of automotive hub of the United States.

00:05:59.020 --> 00:06:02.860
But I wasn’t in Detroit.
I was 30 minutes north on a summer camp,

00:06:02.860 --> 00:06:07.527
working with inner-city underprivileged
kids that happened to be from Detroit.

00:06:07.527 --> 00:06:11.716
And that was a big culture shock for me,
because you know I’m this heavy metal guy

00:06:11.716 --> 00:06:14.741
from Sheffield, North of England.
I’m in the middle of nowhere.

00:06:14.741 --> 00:06:16.876
I had to adapt pretty quickly.

00:06:16.876 --> 00:06:19.466
So I adapted pretty quickly
on this summer camp,

00:06:19.466 --> 00:06:22.855
and when that camp was over,
I got back on that Trailways bus,

00:06:22.855 --> 00:06:27.852
and I took that bus out west,
landed in Los Angeles, 1986,

00:06:27.852 --> 00:06:30.364
Union Station 4 a.m. in the morning.

00:06:30.364 --> 00:06:32.374
I’ve watched all those TV shows,

00:06:32.374 --> 00:06:35.768
but I found myself being awakened
on a park bench at 6 am in the morning

00:06:35.768 --> 00:06:39.116
by a LAPD guy, who told me:
"You can’t sleep here."

00:06:39.116 --> 00:06:40.555
I was a little disappointed.

00:06:40.555 --> 00:06:44.017
I’d seen all these shows in LA
but where were all the beautiful people,

00:06:44.017 --> 00:06:46.307
where were all the rock stars
and movie stars?

00:06:46.307 --> 00:06:48.626
That wasn’t happening in downtown LA.

00:06:48.626 --> 00:06:52.907
But quickly I found my way to Hollywood,
and over the next couple of years,

00:06:52.907 --> 00:06:54.417
I sort of did a few odd jobs.

00:06:54.417 --> 00:06:56.575
But there was one pivotal moment
that happened

00:06:56.575 --> 00:06:59.060
within 3 days of being in Los Angeles.

00:06:59.060 --> 00:07:02.882
I found myself at this YMCA hotel,
right off Hollywood Boulevard.

00:07:02.882 --> 00:07:04.751
I went shopping on Hollywood Boulevard,

00:07:04.751 --> 00:07:09.742
and saw these great PVC alligator print
pants that were on sale for $9.99.

00:07:09.742 --> 00:07:12.839
So I bought myself a pair,
but they didn’t really fit good.

00:07:12.839 --> 00:07:17.026
So I went back to the youth hostel, bought
a sewing kit, and sewed them inside out,

00:07:17.026 --> 00:07:22.284
and decide I’m going to the street that
everyone was talking about called Melrose.

00:07:22.284 --> 00:07:25.676
So I ended up going down there to Melrose,
and walked into this shop

00:07:25.676 --> 00:07:28.315
that was called Retail Slut,
it was a punk rock shop

00:07:28.315 --> 00:07:32.046
and there was a guy working there
that was in a band called Faster Pussycat,

00:07:32.046 --> 00:07:35.322
and his name was Taimie.
Pivotal part to a story here.

00:07:35.322 --> 00:07:37.867
Taimie says to me --
he realized I was from England,

00:07:37.867 --> 00:07:40.948
started a conversation:
“Where did you get those pants from?”

00:07:40.948 --> 00:07:43.335
I said: “Hey, you know,
I got them from England."

00:07:43.335 --> 00:07:47.235
I had to think quick on my feet.
I said: "Why? Do you want to buy them?"

00:07:47.235 --> 00:07:50.853
Just sort of jokingly, and he said,
“Sure. Yeah, how much are they?”

00:07:50.853 --> 00:07:53.471
At this point I hadn’t thought
about selling these pants

00:07:53.471 --> 00:07:56.470
but I said the first number
that came to mind, $25.

00:07:56.470 --> 00:07:58.140
He said, “Okay. I’ll take 8 pairs.”

00:07:58.140 --> 00:08:01.571
So I ran right up to Hollywood Boulevard,
bought 8 pairs of pants,

00:08:01.571 --> 00:08:05.214
went back down,
and sold them to him $15 profit per pant.

00:08:05.214 --> 00:08:09.126
I realized in that one hour transaction,
I’d made more straight away --

00:08:09.126 --> 00:08:12.428
literally within being in LA for 3 days,
than I'd made in a whole week

00:08:12.428 --> 00:08:14.029
working construction in England.

00:08:14.029 --> 00:08:17.307
So I thought, "Maybe LA is a place
for me, it seems pretty easy.

00:08:17.307 --> 00:08:20.088
They speak English,
a lot of rock and roll."

00:08:20.088 --> 00:08:24.609
It was Guns N’ Roses, Motley Crue, it was
a great time over the next few years.

00:08:24.609 --> 00:08:26.179
Fast-forward to 1989.

00:08:26.179 --> 00:08:29.021
I’m selling second-hand clothing
on the Boardwalk in Venice,

00:08:29.021 --> 00:08:31.534
going to yard sales, buying old Levi’s,

00:08:31.534 --> 00:08:35.207
cowboy boots, western shirts.
I’m in the clothing industry now.

00:08:35.207 --> 00:08:37.983
Venice Beach back then was
a major tourist attraction,

00:08:37.983 --> 00:08:40.069
a lot of European people coming through.

00:08:40.069 --> 00:08:44.010
Little by little this grew into a business
which became known as Serious Clothing

00:08:44.010 --> 00:08:46.864
and we ended up outfitting
everyone from Alice Cooper

00:08:46.864 --> 00:08:48.692
to Madonna, and everyone in between.

00:08:48.692 --> 00:08:52.278
We started wholesaling
to a small chain called Hot Topic.

00:08:52.278 --> 00:08:56.969
Back then Hot Topic had five stores,
and would grow to over 500 stores.

00:08:56.969 --> 00:08:59.890
So we sort of went from making
a little amount of clothing

00:08:59.890 --> 00:09:02.222
to making thousands of pieces of clothing.

00:09:02.222 --> 00:09:06.768
Well, in 1994, we realized being in Venice
wasn’t so easy for a clothing company.

00:09:06.768 --> 00:09:11.674
We moved downtown, and rented a loft
in a warehouse for the next 6 years.

00:09:11.674 --> 00:09:14.369
Serious Clothing then started doing
a lot of music videos

00:09:14.369 --> 00:09:19.623
and also a lot of outfits for magazines,
and stylists who call in all the time.

00:09:19.623 --> 00:09:21.839
Serious Clothing had its own unique style.

00:09:21.839 --> 00:09:25.084
We took fabrics that were
not necessarily garment fabrics.

00:09:25.084 --> 00:09:29.154
We used some car seat fabrics, and made
them into jackets, and things like that,

00:09:29.154 --> 00:09:32.769
non-conventional materials,
thinking outside the box,

00:09:32.769 --> 00:09:34.979
and basically doing what we like to wear.

00:09:34.979 --> 00:09:38.720
Well, by 2000, we realized we'd paid
two people’s mortgages and we needed --

00:09:38.720 --> 00:09:40.465
Hey, let’s buy our own building.

00:09:40.465 --> 00:09:42.865
So we ended up finding this building --

00:09:42.865 --> 00:09:45.897
Oh, that was me back then,
forgot that little picture.

00:09:45.897 --> 00:09:50.499
So that was me pre-beard,
that’s sort of circa 1994.

00:09:50.499 --> 00:09:53.648
Serious was one of the top 10
clothing companies to watch.

00:09:53.648 --> 00:09:57.811
So anyway, 2000, my wife Karen
found this building in the Arts District.

00:09:57.811 --> 00:10:00.290
People said, "You’re crazy,
no one wants to be there,

00:10:00.290 --> 00:10:02.070
former desolate industrial area."

00:10:02.070 --> 00:10:04.458
Long story short,
we took another leap of faith.

00:10:04.458 --> 00:10:06.172
Our gut feeling felt good.

00:10:06.172 --> 00:10:09.062
Why we’re paying two people’s mortgages

00:10:09.062 --> 00:10:12.987
when we could own our own building.
So we bought that building.

00:10:12.987 --> 00:10:16.081
About a year later,
right after 9/11 in 2001,

00:10:16.081 --> 00:10:19.287
there was an article in the LA Times,
about loft gentrification.

00:10:19.287 --> 00:10:21.854
We got a phone call,
would we be interested in renting

00:10:21.854 --> 00:10:23.660
the building for a music video?

00:10:23.660 --> 00:10:27.186
Bang! Before you know it,
we’re in the film location business.

00:10:27.186 --> 00:10:32.044
Well, hey, we’ve been filming scenes
since 2001, over 100 days a year,

00:10:32.044 --> 00:10:35.769
doing things from low-budget still shoots,
to big-budget movies,

00:10:35.769 --> 00:10:40.806
and over a dozen reality shows
like America’s Next Top Model.

00:10:40.806 --> 00:10:45.387
We met a lot of interesting people, but we
didn’t plan to build a film location.

00:10:45.387 --> 00:10:49.409
We were building our dream,
live-work house, where we lived upstairs

00:10:49.409 --> 00:10:52.366
and operated our clothing company
out of the downstairs.

00:10:52.366 --> 00:10:55.927
So we’d accidentally fallen into another
somewhat lucrative business.

00:10:55.927 --> 00:10:59.888
This is LA, it’s movie town, we’ve met
quite a lot of interesting people.

00:10:59.888 --> 00:11:01.838
They always say: "How did you get here?"

00:11:01.838 --> 00:11:04.143
Well, we tell them,
we followed our gut feeling.

00:11:04.143 --> 00:11:08.427
Remember that story, I was a 10-year-old
when I fell in love with Porsche.

00:11:10.497 --> 00:11:12.762
Fell in love with Porsche
as a 10-year-old.

00:11:12.762 --> 00:11:15.368
I didn’t buy my first Porsche till 1992.

00:11:15.368 --> 00:11:19.448
Serious Clothing had become
quite successful, and from ‘92 to 2000,

00:11:19.448 --> 00:11:22.384
I was racing around getting
quite a lot of speeding tickets.

00:11:22.384 --> 00:11:26.081
2001, I took my aggressive
street driving to the track,

00:11:26.081 --> 00:11:29.762
and joined the Porsche Owners Club.
I went through their program.

00:11:29.762 --> 00:11:33.658
I learned how to do club racing,
instructing, and for the next 5 years

00:11:33.658 --> 00:11:36.197
was doing 50 track days a year.

00:11:36.197 --> 00:11:41.369
Turn around to probably 2008-2009, I'd
spent a lot of money racing and decided,

00:11:41.369 --> 00:11:43.920
"Okay, my next passion: I love these cars.

00:11:43.920 --> 00:11:45.950
Why don’t I try and restore
a few of them?"

00:11:45.950 --> 00:11:49.312
Well, I didn’t have any
mechanical background, but I had passion.

00:11:49.312 --> 00:11:52.461
I often say that passion
goes a long, long way.

00:11:52.461 --> 00:11:56.127
You know, if you’ve got the will,
and the desire, and put the motivation in,

00:11:56.127 --> 00:11:58.613
and focus, things tend to happen.

00:11:58.613 --> 00:12:02.449
Also a little bit of luck, and a leap
of faith really help out as well.

00:12:02.449 --> 00:12:06.613
But I asked a lot of questions,
and I started restoring a couple of cars.

00:12:06.613 --> 00:12:09.881
So I got a little bit of interest
in European car magazines,

00:12:09.881 --> 00:12:11.866
and I started this blog online.

00:12:11.866 --> 00:12:15.597
Well, there is a thread on this Porsche
forum called Pelican Parts.

00:12:15.597 --> 00:12:18.915
And I called my blog
Porsche Collection Out Of Control Hobby.

00:12:18.915 --> 00:12:21.802
And I was sort of like a catch-all
of what I was doing.

00:12:21.802 --> 00:12:24.493
And so this was sort of going
to become a pivotal point

00:12:24.493 --> 00:12:27.688
where it was like something I really
really enjoyed to do.

00:12:27.688 --> 00:12:29.883
And I’d start restoring these cars.

00:12:29.883 --> 00:12:35.131
Well, about 2 years ago, a pivotal moment
in our life happened again.

00:12:35.131 --> 00:12:40.800
About every 10 years these pivotal moments
seem to happen by accident,

00:12:40.800 --> 00:12:42.644
or they’re just naturally evolving.

00:12:42.644 --> 00:12:46.269
We never had this five,
ten-year planned business model.

00:12:46.269 --> 00:12:50.402
We always go back to follow your gut,
do what you love to do.

00:12:50.402 --> 00:12:54.028
So having been in the film industry,
we got quite a lot of people interested

00:12:54.028 --> 00:12:56.352
in making little TV shows,
and stuff like that,

00:12:56.352 --> 00:12:58.432
but we weren’t quite ready
for the exposure,

00:12:58.432 --> 00:13:03.363
or the compatibility wasn’t quite right,
or it just didn’t click.

00:13:03.363 --> 00:13:06.303
So I got a call from this Canadian
called Tamir Moscovici.

00:13:06.303 --> 00:13:09.779
Well, he’d seen a couple of articles,
and he was a film director,

00:13:09.779 --> 00:13:13.460
also a Porsche guy, and he was looking
for something edgy for his reel.

00:13:13.460 --> 00:13:16.766
He was sort of sick of doing Bud Light
commercials and figured, hey,

00:13:16.766 --> 00:13:19.786
maybe there’s more of the Magnus’ story
than meets the eye.

00:13:19.786 --> 00:13:24.765
So we had a couple of conversations,
and Tamir ended up flying down to LA,

00:13:24.765 --> 00:13:29.241
little over two years ago, on his frequent
flyer miles, a complete leap of faith.

00:13:29.241 --> 00:13:33.161
His original idea was to make
a short YouTube documentary.

00:13:33.161 --> 00:13:36.433
Well, our thought was, "What’s the worst
that could happen here?

00:13:36.433 --> 00:13:39.931
We’ll drive around, race around
in my favorite Porsches for 4 days,

00:13:39.931 --> 00:13:41.958
and maybe get
some good footage out of it."

00:13:41.958 --> 00:13:47.311
Well, what turned out to be a 32-minute
documentary was shot over 4 days.

00:13:47.311 --> 00:13:53.230
So we shot, I think, in February of 2012,
and we released a trailer in June of 2012.

00:13:53.230 --> 00:13:55.543
We didn’t know
what would happen with the trailer

00:13:55.543 --> 00:13:58.623
but somehow it got picked up
by Top Gear and within the first day

00:13:58.623 --> 00:14:02.090
it got over 50,000 views,
and all of a sudden I’d just found

00:14:02.090 --> 00:14:04.974
this thing called Facebook.
I figured I should get on that.

00:14:04.974 --> 00:14:06.637
I didn’t really know much about it.

00:14:06.637 --> 00:14:09.827
So I got on Facebook, and at this time
I don’t even have an iPhone.

00:14:09.827 --> 00:14:12.481
So I’m not really internet savvy,
but all of a sudden,

00:14:12.481 --> 00:14:16.161
I keep getting all these friend requests
from all these oddball places,

00:14:16.161 --> 00:14:19.962
you know, Spain and Indonesia,
and I’m thinking what’s going on.

00:14:19.962 --> 00:14:24.126
Well, this trailer for that 3-minute film,
Urban Outlaw, that Top Gear picked up,

00:14:24.126 --> 00:14:28.011
it got blogged, reblogged and reblogged.
Well, this was pretty exciting.

00:14:28.011 --> 00:14:31.092
So this was a leap of faith project,
everyone was sort of working

00:14:31.092 --> 00:14:34.787
on a shoestring budget,
bro down buddy favorite type of thing,

00:14:34.787 --> 00:14:37.308
and they were doing this
sort of on the side.

00:14:37.308 --> 00:14:40.764
So, little by little I started posting
the film was going to come out.

00:14:40.764 --> 00:14:43.963
Well, to me being sort
of a production type of guy,

00:14:43.963 --> 00:14:46.764
we shopped it around a few film festivals.

00:14:46.764 --> 00:14:49.817
Somehow it got into this thing
called the Raindance Film Festival

00:14:49.817 --> 00:14:54.573
which I described as the rainy version
of Sundance, that’s in England.

00:14:54.573 --> 00:14:59.253
I’m from England, what are the chances
that you get to premiere your film

00:14:59.253 --> 00:15:01.707
in front of an audience similar to this?

00:15:01.707 --> 00:15:05.560
So Karen and I flew to London, and we
premiered the film in Piccadilly Circus

00:15:05.560 --> 00:15:08.377
on a Saturday night
around 10 o’clock, and it sold out.

00:15:08.377 --> 00:15:10.495
There was a buzz about this film.

00:15:10.495 --> 00:15:13.407
Well, we decided, "Okay,
we’re going to release it online."

00:15:13.407 --> 00:15:17.460
So October 15, the film went online,
and probably 2 weeks after it came out,

00:15:17.460 --> 00:15:19.743
I got a phone call from Jay Leno.

00:15:19.743 --> 00:15:23.043
Jay Leno had seen the film,
and wanted it to be on his garage show.

00:15:23.043 --> 00:15:26.875
Well, that started the Avalanche of what
has happened for the past 18 months.

00:15:26.875 --> 00:15:29.785
All of a sudden, this is my life before
Urban Outlaw came out,

00:15:29.785 --> 00:15:31.352
and this is my life after.

00:15:31.352 --> 00:15:34.774
Now at this point, we’ve been doing
Serious Clothing for 20 years,

00:15:34.774 --> 00:15:37.316
and we weren’t quite as motivated
as we once were.

00:15:37.316 --> 00:15:40.536
You know, we always said we design
what we personally like to wear,

00:15:40.536 --> 00:15:43.597
but over the past few years
we’ve sort of been treading water.

00:15:43.597 --> 00:15:46.959
So we took this leap of faith,
and decided success

00:15:46.959 --> 00:15:49.902
really is the freedom
to do whatever you want to do.

00:15:49.902 --> 00:15:52.756
So we decided we were going
to close Serious down.

00:15:52.756 --> 00:15:56.327
This was the baby that had enabled us
to get to this point.

00:15:56.327 --> 00:16:00.557
Now it wasn’t like we gave up on Serious,
we still had all the patents and samples.

00:16:00.557 --> 00:16:03.950
But what it did was once we decided
to pull that band aid off,

00:16:03.950 --> 00:16:05.760
it allowed us some breathing room.

00:16:05.760 --> 00:16:09.863
We didn’t know what was coming next, but
we knew it was going to be something good.

00:16:09.863 --> 00:16:13.481
So once we closed that door,
probably in the past 18 months

00:16:13.481 --> 00:16:19.239
I’ve probably done a hundred
magazine video TV show interviews.

00:16:19.239 --> 00:16:23.673
I think by closing Serious’ door,
it opened up all this freedom to travel.

00:16:23.673 --> 00:16:27.268
Remember me telling about Porsche,
and that letter I wrote as a 10-year-old.

00:16:27.268 --> 00:16:30.921
About a month after the film came out,
I received a letter from Porsche.

00:16:30.921 --> 00:16:34.923
Basically they’d seen the film and was
sort of impressed with my Porsche passion,

00:16:34.923 --> 00:16:38.153
and realized they’d written me
a letter 35 years later.

00:16:38.153 --> 00:16:40.038
Ironically in the film, Tamir asked me:

00:16:40.038 --> 00:16:42.822
"What do you think Porsche would think
about you doing it?"

00:16:42.822 --> 00:16:45.861
I said: "I don’t know. I hope
they’d be smiling and be happy."

00:16:45.861 --> 00:16:47.824
So Porsche wrote me a second letter.

00:16:47.824 --> 00:16:50.767
I wish I had the first one,
but I do have the second one.

00:16:50.767 --> 00:16:53.497
They invited me to go visit
them in Stuttgart,

00:16:53.497 --> 00:16:55.947
and tour the museum which I went to do.

00:16:55.947 --> 00:17:00.580
Purely by coincidence,
I had been there on 9/11 2013.

00:17:00.580 --> 00:17:02.818
Well after that
there was at the LA Auto Show.

00:17:02.818 --> 00:17:04.790
We did a couple of events with Porsche.

00:17:04.790 --> 00:17:07.535
Hosted these events
in my garage in downtown LA.

00:17:07.535 --> 00:17:11.092
It was a worldwide dealer event,
it brought all their dealers over,

00:17:11.092 --> 00:17:14.393
and incorporated me into this workshop
where Porsche was talking about

00:17:14.393 --> 00:17:17.193
what Porsche does restoration,
and Porsche classic.

00:17:17.193 --> 00:17:19.834
I think they sensed I have this thing --

00:17:19.834 --> 00:17:21.654
Porsche passion is what I said it was,

00:17:21.654 --> 00:17:24.034
and it’s something that
you can’t really build,

00:17:24.034 --> 00:17:27.198
and you can’t market, and you can sell it.
It’s just sort of there.

00:17:27.198 --> 00:17:31.328
So from then Porsche integrated me into
this workshop, invited me out to an event

00:17:31.328 --> 00:17:35.928
in Essen, Germany, and basically started
to invite me out to places,

00:17:35.928 --> 00:17:39.180
and incorporate me into
their commercials coming up.

00:17:39.180 --> 00:17:42.444
The Porsche connection was quite simple,
but what we hadn’t expected

00:17:42.444 --> 00:17:46.399
but also came from the film,
was we got approached by Nike.

00:17:46.399 --> 00:17:49.240
We got approached by Oakley,
and then we had a visit

00:17:49.240 --> 00:17:54.803
from Bentley chief designer, and we
also had a visit from BMW, and Volvo.

00:17:54.803 --> 00:17:56.943
It’s almost like these people
were thinking

00:17:56.943 --> 00:18:00.062
I was some sort of focus group,
and they were asking my opinion

00:18:00.062 --> 00:18:02.924
on what did I think
about certain things.

00:18:02.924 --> 00:18:05.327
I’m scratching my head
a little bit thinking:

00:18:05.327 --> 00:18:08.226
"I’m just a guy doing my own thing,"
but you know

00:18:08.226 --> 00:18:10.051
people seem to have responded to it.

00:18:10.051 --> 00:18:13.810
Well, I get a lot of emails from people
who talk often about the video,

00:18:13.810 --> 00:18:17.134
and the greatest thing I suppose
separate from people liking the cars

00:18:17.134 --> 00:18:21.987
is the fact that people found the film,
and my story inspirational.

00:18:21.987 --> 00:18:26.073
So if there’s one message
I can leave you with, for me --

00:18:26.073 --> 00:18:29.657
what I’ve done over the past 28 years
involved a lot of leaps of faith,

00:18:29.657 --> 00:18:32.969
always going on my gut feeling
when things sort of seemed awkward,

00:18:32.969 --> 00:18:36.448
that was often the case to know,
"Hey we’re on the right track here,"

00:18:36.448 --> 00:18:40.625
and just stay motivated, stay dedicated.
We never asked anyone’s opinion.

00:18:40.625 --> 00:18:43.930
We just did what we like to do,
and it seems to have worked out

00:18:43.930 --> 00:18:46.309
quite nice for us.
We don’t know where we’re going.

00:18:46.309 --> 00:18:49.729
I often say I’m on this open road,
along for the ride.

00:18:49.729 --> 00:18:52.786
We’ll see what comes next,
but I really appreciate all your time,

00:18:52.786 --> 00:18:56.267
and allowing me to share my story.
Cheers and all the best.

00:18:56.267 --> 00:18:57.858
(Cheering)

00:18:57.858 --> 00:19:02.458
(Applause)

